17] [17592186357304 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357304 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357304 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357304 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357304 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186355795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357304 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d75ae775-a251-534e-9f9e-8ed3717f8746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357504 #edb/ref 1069 "q-91174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357504 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357504 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -4 & 14 \\\\ 8 & 2 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -2 & 8 \\\\ 11 & -1 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-26 & 0 & -10 & -10 \\\\ 30 & 0 & 0 & 22 \\\\ -52 & 0 & -12 & -32 \\\\ 11 & -1 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\nI. ${R}_{1}:=…$\n\nII. ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{4}$\n\nIII. ${R}_{3}:=…$\n\neach of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{4},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ccf8865b-925d-5ab2-8a04-26b4740f798d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357504 #edb/ref 1074 "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries above ${a}_{42}=-1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -4 & 14 \\\\ 8 & 2 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -2 & 8 \\\\ 11 & -1 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-26 & 0 & -10 & -10 \\\\ 8 & 2 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -2 & 8 \\\\ 11 & -1 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-26 & 0 & -10 & -10 \\\\ 30 & 0 & 0 & 22 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -2 & 8 \\\\ 11 & -1 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-5{R}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-26 & 0 & -10 & -10 \\\\ 30 & 0 & 0 & 22 \\\\ -52 & 0 & -12 & -32 \\\\ 11 & -1 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{4}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-5{R}_{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357688 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357688 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94194" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357688 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357688 #edb/ref 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(-1.2<Z<-0.31).$\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1151\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1131\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1112\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1357\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1335\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1314\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1587\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1539\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3085\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3050\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3015\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3446\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3409\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3372\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3821\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3783\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3745\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357688 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4f8c403-ca6a-51f4-8127-1029a77ae1d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357688 #edb/ref 1074 "We're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(-1.2<Z<-0.31).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46b71217fcee1d130b3778ce2cd101aac9814870b71683656987d476e71412c1.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.2<Z<-0.31) & =P(Z<-0.31)-P(Z\\le -1.2) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.31)-P(Z\\le -1.2) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.31)-Φ(-1.2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.2) & =0.1151 \\\\ Φ(-0.31) & =0.3783\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.2<Z<-0.31) & =Φ(-0.31)-Φ(-1.2) \\\\  & =0.3783-0.1151 \\\\  & =0.2632.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357729 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357729 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94327" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357729 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357729 #edb/ref 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(4,{6}^{2}),$ compute $P(-3.2<X<2.86).$\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0968\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0951\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0934\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1151\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1131\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1112\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1357\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1335\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1314\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3557\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3520\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3483\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3936\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3897\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3859\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4325\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4286\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4247\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357729 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8683cc2-a956-58b3-9029-b018ebaed6ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357729 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)=P(\\frac{a-\\mu }{\\sigma }<Z<\\frac{b-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =4$ and $\\sigma =6.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3.2<X<2.86) & =P(\\frac{-3.2-4}{6}<Z<\\frac{2.86-4}{6}) \\\\  & =P(-1.2<Z<-0.19)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/1e/1ed2c43f507a4d0ddd54126bcd3efaa2fa9b56c63ea908e8567e6959e3fc67bc.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.2<Z<-0.19) & =P(Z<-0.19)-P(Z\\le -1.2) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.19)-P(Z\\le -1.2) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.19)-Φ(-1.2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.2) & =0.1151 \\\\ Φ(-0.19) & =0.4247\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.2<Z<-0.19) & =Φ(-0.19)-Φ(-1.2) \\\\  & =0.4247-0.1151 \\\\  & =0.3096.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(-3.2<X<2.86)=0.3096.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357775 #edb/ref 1080 "0.0556" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357775 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357775 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "022d673b-fe73-5da1-8708-a3a7e5646a4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357853 #edb/ref 1080 "0.2287" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357853 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17